    {"title": "What to do with a topological picture",
     "layout": "text", "size": 26,
     "body": ["Ask which representation carries the claim: the diagram, the graph, or the drawing",
              "Treat Mapper structure as lens-, cover-, **clusterer- and scale-**dependent until shown otherwise for your data",
              "Use a null that shares the construction (surrogates), not a different representation",
              "State thresholds as conventions; report distributions over resamples, not one reference",
              "A graph cycle is not a loop, and a missing cycle is not a missing loop"],
     "notes": """So what should a practitioner do? Ask which representation carries the claim. Treat Mapper structure as dependent on lens, cover, clusterer and scale until shown otherwise. Use a null control that shares the construction, such as surrogates, rather than a different representation. State thresholds as conventions and report distributions over resamples rather than one reference. And remember both directions: a graph cycle is not a loop in the data, and a missing cycle does not show a missing loop. I do not claim that people routinely confuse these. I claim the two kinds of evidence are not interchangeable."""},

